ing in  in a programming language rather than  making everything continuous  now now the second component here is  actually we want these systems to  improve over time  i won't have too much time to go into 2h  details here but  but even in the previous system actually  we will see that since we are training  these models again and again as new data  is coming in  or we are generalizing them to slightly  different dsms on top of what they  already have  so we do see this type of transfer where  we don't have to train these systems  again and again from scratch we can just  leverage  what we have trained and which captures  this notion of as humans also when we  learn programming we don't just  forget everything we have learned in the  past we can keep building on top  i'll be happy to talk about it later in  the q a if this time  about this as well now the next next  part was  understanding multimodal specifications  uh and for this i'm going to use this  example called tf coding  uh so df query is the system where  it's similar to smart phil where users  can give  introductory examples of tensor  transformations they want to perform in  tensorflow  uh but the interesting part here is that  in addition to examples they can also  specify their intent  in natural language so here's an example  uh taken from stack overflow where  a user specifies uh a task they have in  in english  and then they also give an example to  clarify what sort of tasks  they have in mind uh and actually this  was a question for which there was no  answer  for i think almost two days and when we  ran the system uh actually this  uh answer was generated by tf coded  and uh there are many such uh problems  for which  df coder can generate uh exam uh  generate answers which are  which are quite uh quite some uh similar  to how  human experts would write uh so the  basic idea of the system is that  we have a natural language specification  and then we have some important examples  and then we give this type of  specification to the system  and then the system comes up with a  program uh that  is hopefully uh that's guaranteed to be  consistent with the examples and also  uh likely consistent with the with the  natural language specification  um so so the basic architecture is  similar to  robust film we saw earlier where we have  we're going to build these models that  can  ingest examples to distributions over  programs  uh one in interesting thing here is that  we also have natural language  specification in addition to examples  uh but the nice part about having these  uh  learning based mechanisms is that um for  for these actually we can just have one  more encoder and we can keep  the whole pipeline similar to as before  so so that's the nice thing about having  a learning based system that we can  add new and new modalities or  specifications  uh just as easily uh in our synthesis  process  now one challenge uh unlike that unlike  improper examples is that we can't  synthetically generate lots and lots of  uh natural language we can but it won't  be very realistic  and it won't be um the distribution  won't match the kind of distribution we  see  at this time from users so for  learning a language to api distribution  part  uh we for this work we leverage the  documentation  that these apis have uh and then we do  some  pfidf based cosine similarity to say  given a natural language statement  which apis are most likely to be likely  to be relevant and useful  so once we have these two distributions  from examples in natural language to  events  we can then perform this type of  intuitive uh search we were  looking at earlier where we have  programs of  different apis a composition of  different aps and we can guide them  using  using these weights and distributions  and and and again here also in some of  the preliminary experiments we were  doing we did see that  not only not only um by adding this  learning component we can  improve search based sy